conversation:
user: I'm working on a volley ball script for fivem 
I'm trying to create a NUI allowing players to select teams 
could you show me an example of how I could make something that looks like the image attached
https://cdn.discordapp.com/attachments/1052780891300184096/1095516909283328072/image.png
assistant: First, you'll need to create an HTML file for your NUI, then add some CSS for the styling and a bit of JavaScript to handle team selection logic.

Here is a simple example of how your NUI HTML file can look like:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Team Selection</title>
    <style>
        body { 
            background-color: rgba(0,0,0,0.8); 
            display: flex; 
            justify-content: center; 
            align-items: center; 
            height: 100vh; 
            margin: 0; 
        }
        .container { 
            background-color: white; 
            border-radius: 5px; 
            padding: 20px; 
            display: flex; 
            justify-content: space-around; 
            align-items: center; 
            width: 60%; 
        }
        .team { 
            display: flex; 
            flex-direction: column; 
            align-items: center; 
        }
        .team img { 
            width: 200px; 
            height: 200px; 
            margin-bottom: 10px; 
            border-radius: 50%; 
        }
        .team button { 
            background-color: #008CBA; 
            border: none; 
            color: white; 
            padding: 10px 20px; 
            text-align: center; 
            text-decoration: none; 
            display: inline-block; 
            font-size: 16px; 
            margin: 10px 2px; 
            cursor: pointer; 
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="team">
            <img src="team-a-logo.png" alt="Team A">
            <button onclick="selectTeam('A')">Join Team A</button>
        </div>
        <div class="team">
            <img src="team-b-logo.png" alt="Team B">
            <button onclick="selectTeam('B')">Join Team B</button>
        </div>
    </div>
    <script>
        function selectTeam(teamName) {
            // You can use the FiveM JavaScript API here to send the selected team to your script.
            console.log("Player selected Team: " + teamName);

            // Example using the FiveM API:
            // SendNuiMessage(JSON.stringify({ type: "teamSelection", team: teamName }));
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

In the example above, replace `team-a-logo.png` and `team-b-logo.png` with appropriate images for your team logos.

For this NUI, you can trigger it to be shown/hidden using FiveM's NUI API (https://docs.fivem.net/docs/scripting-reference/nui/native-api/).

In your client-side Lua script, you can listen for the NUI message and handle the team selection. Here's a simple example:

```lua
-- Listen for NUI messages
RegisterNUICallback('teamSelection', function(data, cb)
    local team = data.team

    if team == 'A' then
        -- Add the player to Team A
    elseif team == 'B' then
        -- Add the player to Team B
    end

    -- Hide the NUI
    SetNuiFocus(false, false)

    cb('ok') -- Respond to the NUI message
end)

-- When starting the resource, show the NUI:
CreateThread(function()
  Wait(1000)
  SetNuiFocus(true, true)
  SendNUIMessage({type = 'showTeamSelection'})
end)
```

This is a basic example of how to create a team selection NUI simillar to the image provided. You can adjust the CSS and JavaScript as needed to fit your desired appearance and behavior.
user: can you show me a guide to making my first NUI
assistant: Certainly! Here's a simple guide to help you get started with creating your first NUI in FiveM.

1. Create a new resource folder:

Create a new folder for your resource in your server's resource directory. For example, create the folder `my_first_nui` in `resources`.

2. Create the resource manifest file:

Create a `fxmanifest.lua` file inside the `my_first_nui` folder with the following content:

```lua
fx_version 'cerulean'
game 'gta5'

author 'Your Name'
description 'My First NUI Sample'
version '1.0.0'

-- Define the NUI index file
ui_page 'html/index.html'

-- Specify client-side script file
client_script 'client.lua'

-- Include your HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and other files
files {
  'html/index.html',
  'html/style.css',
  'html/script.js'
}
```

3. Create the NUI files:

Create an `html` folder inside the `my_first_nui` folder. Inside the `html` folder, create three separate files: `index.html`, `style.css`, and `script.js`.

4. Set up basic HTML structure:

Edit the `index.html` file with a simple HTML structure.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>My First NUI</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Hello, NUI!</h1>

  <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

5. Apply some CSS styling:

Edit the `style.css` file to add some custom styling.

```css
body {
  background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.8);
  color: white;
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
  height: 100vh;
  margin: 0;
}

h1 {
  font-size: 3rem;
}
```

6. Add JavaScript:

Edit the `script.js` file to add some JavaScript actions. For this example, we are not adding any code, but this is where you would add the JavaScript required for your UI.

7. Create a client-side script:

Create a `client.lua` file inside the `my_first_nui` folder. Add the following code which will show the NUI when the player types `/shownui` in the in-game chat.

```lua
RegisterCommand('shownui', function()
  -- Show the NUI
  SetNuiFocus(true, true)
  SendNUIMessage({ action = 'show' })
end)
```

8. Start your resource:

Add the line `start my_first_nui` to your server configuration file (`server.cfg`) and start your server. Test the NUI by typing `/shownui` in the in-game chat. You should see the "Hello, NUI!" message displayed in your game.

That's it! You've created your first NUI resource in FiveM. You can now build more complex NUI interfaces by adding more HTML, CSS, and JavaScript as needed.
user: can you use ‘show’ rather then 'show' in lua?
assistant: Yes, you can use double quotes (`"`) instead of single quotes (`'`) to define strings in Lua. Both are valid in Lua, and you can choose either one according to your personal preference or coding style.

Here's the previous example using double quotes instead of single quotes:

```lua
RegisterCommand("shownui", function()
  -- Show the NUI
  SetNuiFocus(true, true)
  SendNUIMessage({ action = "show" })
end)
```

Remember to maintain consistency throughout your code, as mixing single and double quotes for strings might make your code harder to read and maintain.
user: this creates a NUI but it shows all the time and the shownui command doesn't do anything
assistant: Apologies for the confusion. To ensure that the NUI initially remains hidden, you'll need to modify your CSS and JavaScript files. Follow the steps below to correct this issue:

1. Edit your `style.css` file and add the following CSS rule to hide the NUI content by default:

```css
#nui-wrapper {
  display: none;
}
```

2. Update the `index.html` file to wrap the content inside a div with the `id` attribute set to `nui-wrapper`:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>My First NUI</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>
  <div id="nui-wrapper">
    <h1>Hello, NUI!</h1>
  </div>
  <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

3. Modify the `script.js` to listen for NUI messages sent from your client-side Lua script and add a function to show the NUI when the `/shownui` command is used:

```javascript
window.addEventListener('message', (event) => {
  if (event.data.action === 'show') {
    document.getElementById('nui-wrapper').style.display = 'block';
  }
});
```

Now, when you run the `/shownui` command, the NUI should appear on the screen as it's set to be hidden by default. The command then triggers a message that causes the NUI content to be displayed.
user: could you generate a preview of what the html outcome would look like and share a link so i can view the image
assistant: As an AI language model, I cannot directly generate an image or share a link to a live preview. However, I can provide you with an alternative method for previewing your HTML code.

To preview your HTML, CSS, and JavaScript code, you can use online code editors, such as JSFiddle, CodeSandbox, or CodePen.

Here's an example containing the code you provided, which is pasted into a new JSFiddle:

1. Visit https://jsfiddle.net/
2. In the HTML panel, copy and paste the code from your `index.html` file.
3. In the CSS panel, copy and paste the code from your `style.css` file.
4. In the JavaScript panel, copy and paste the code from your `script.js` file.
5. Click "Run" in the top navigation menu to view your code in the "Result" panel.

Please note that this method will only provide you with a general preview of how the code appears. Since it's running outside the FiveM environment, it won't respond to triggers and events inside the game. Nevertheless, it is a useful method for quickly checking the appearance of your NUI before implementing it in FiveM.

